    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is WordPress better than Webflow?</h3>
    <p>WordPress is better for users who need maximum flexibility, plugin ecosystem access, ecommerce at scale, and full ownership of their site through self-hosting. Webflow is better for designers and marketing teams who want pixel-perfect visual control, managed hosting, and clean semantic code without touching a backend.</p>

    <h3>Is WordPress free or do I have to pay?</h3>
    <p>The WordPress software itself is free and open source, but running a live site costs money. You pay for hosting (typically $3 to $25 per month for shared hosting, more for managed WordPress hosting), a domain name (around $10 to $15 per year), and optionally premium themes or plugins. Webflow charges a monthly subscription starting at $14 for the Basic plan, which includes hosting.</p>

    <h3>Which is better for SEO, WordPress or Webflow?</h3>
    <p>Both can produce excellent SEO results when configured correctly. WordPress relies on SEO plugins like Yoast or Rank Math for meta tags, sitemaps, and schema, which gives you fine control but requires maintenance. Webflow handles clean semantic HTML, automatic sitemaps, and meta controls natively without plugins, which is simpler but less customizable. For most users, Webflow's built-in SEO is easier to manage; for advanced SEO professionals, WordPress with the right plugins offers deeper control.</p>

    <h3>Can I build an ecommerce store with WordPress or Webflow?</h3>
    <p>Yes, both platforms support ecommerce. WordPress powers ecommerce through the WooCommerce plugin, which is free, open source, and highly customizable, making it suitable for stores of any size. Webflow Ecommerce is built into the platform and is easier to design visually, but it has higher transaction fees on lower plans and is better suited for smaller catalogs and design-led brands.</p>

    <h3>Do I need to know how to code to use WordPress or Webflow?</h3>
    <p>Neither requires coding for basic use. WordPress lets you build a site with themes and page builders like Elementor without touching code, but customizing themes or fixing plugin conflicts often requires PHP, HTML, and CSS knowledge. Webflow's visual editor produces real HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, so understanding web fundamentals helps you use it effectively, but you never write code directly.</p>

    <h3>Which platform has better hosting and security?</h3>
    <p>Webflow includes managed hosting with SSL, CDN, and DDoS protection on every plan, which means you never patch servers or worry about downtime. WordPress requires you to arrange hosting yourself, whether shared, VPS, or managed WordPress hosting, and you are responsible for updates, backups, and security hardening. For users who want zero maintenance, Webflow wins; for users who want full server control, WordPress wins.</p>

